<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-1-motivation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Motivation for Sequences and Series</title>

  <p> 
    In this chapter we will be working towards one of the most important ideas from calculus: approximating functions via power series.
    To see why this is such an important idea, let us start with the following question: How can we compute <m>\sin(7^{circ})</m>? The angle here
    is mostly irrelevant, but so far we only know how to compute standard trigonometric functions for a couple of angles, and only because we know the angles
    for 3 right triangles (the <m>30-60-90</m>, the <m>45-45-90</m>, and a degenerate triangle). Admittedly, there are also clever tricks to compute 
    some other quantities (such as <m>\sin(15^{circ})</m>) by using angle sum and difference formulas, but this is incredibly difficult, and does not help us 
    answer the original question. 
  </p>
  <p>
    So what approaches can we take? Perhaps you might recall that <m>\lim_{x \to 0}\frac{\sin x}{x} = 1</m>, where <m>x</m> is measured in radians. 
    Another way to state this is that for small angles, <m>\sin x \approx x</m>, represented graphically below.

    <figure xml:id="fig-sin-x-approx">
      <caption>A Linear Approximation to <m>\sin x</m></caption>
      <image>
        <shortdescription></shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \draw[->] (-3, 0) -- (3, 0) node[right] {$x$}; % x-axis
            \draw[->] (0, -3) -- (0, 3) node[above] {$y$}; % y-axis
            \draw[smooth, domain=-3:3, samples=100] plot (\x, {sin (\x r)}); % plot y = sin x
            \draw[color=red,smooth, domain=-3:3, samples=100] plot (\x, {\x}); % plot y = x
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    Also, since <m>\sin(0)=0</m> and <m>\sin'(0)=\cos(0)=1</m>, the line  <m>y = x</m> is the best linear approximation to <m>y = \sin x</m> at <m>x=0</m>.
    With this in mind, we might hope that by considering more general polynomial approximations, we could get a better approximation than just a line. If you try and do this yourself, 
    you may end up finding the polynomial <m>x-\frac{1}{6}x^3</m>, which is graphed below.

    <figure xml:id="fig-sin-x-approx-3rd-order">
      <caption>A Third Order Approximation to <m>\sin x</m></caption>
      <image>
        <shortdescription></shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \draw[->] (-3, 0) -- (3, 0) node[right] {$x$}; % x-axis
            \draw[->] (0, -3) -- (0, 3) node[above] {$y$}; % y-axis
            \draw[smooth, domain=-3:3, samples=100] plot (\x, {sin (\x r)}); % plot y = sin x
            \draw[color=red,smooth, domain=-3:3, samples=100] plot (\x, {\x-\x^3/6}); % plot y = x - x^3/6
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    As we can see above, this approximates <m>\sin x</m> well for even longer than before. We can imagine continuing the process, and in this
    case we can add arbitrarily many terms and get arbitrarily good approximations to <m>\sin x</m> for any <m>x</m>.
  </p>

  <interactive desmos="8fj7gtqwy2" width="60%" aspect="2:3" />

  <p>
    Does the process above work just for <m>\sin x</m>? Absolutely not! In fact, we can use the same idea to approximate many other functions by polynomials, and
    these approximations are known as Taylor (or Maclaurin) series. To see another example, let us look at the function <m>e^x</m>. As a first approximation, we
    can resort to the tangent line at <m>x=0</m>. Since <m>e^0 = 1</m> and <m>(e^x)'\big|_{x=0} = e^0 = 1</m>, the tangent line is <m>y = 1 + x</m>. 
    
    <figure xml:id="fig-e-x-approx-1st-order">
      <caption>Graph of <m>e^x</m> and its tangent line approximation at <m>x=0</m>.</caption>
      <image>
        <shortdescription></shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \draw[->] (-3, 0) -- (3, 0) node[right] {$x$}; % x-axis
            \draw[->] (0, -3) -- (0, 3) node[above] {$y$}; % y-axis
            \draw[smooth, domain=-3:1.1, samples=100] plot (\x, {exp(\x)}); % plot y = e^x
            \draw[color=red,smooth, domain=-3:3, samples=100] plot (\x, {1 + \x}); % plot tan line for e^x
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    By doing this same procedure as we did for <m>\sin x</m>, the sixth degree polynomial that we get is:
    <md>1 + x + \frac{1}{2}x^2 + \frac{1}{6}x^3+\frac{1}{24}x^4+\frac{1}{120}x^5+\frac{1}{720}x^6.</md>

    <figure xml:id="fig-e-x-approx-6th-order">
      <caption>Graph of <m>e^x</m> and its sixth degree Taylor polynomial approximation at <m>x=0</m>.</caption>
      <image>
        <shortdescription></shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
          \draw[->] (-3, 0) -- (3, 0) node[right] {$x$}; % x-axis
          \draw[->] (0, -3) -- (0, 3) node[above] {$y$}; % y-axis
          \draw[smooth, domain=-3:1.1, samples=100] plot (\x, {exp(\x)}); % plot y = e^x
          \draw[color=red,smooth, domain=-3:1.1, samples=100] plot (\x, {1 + \x + \x*\x/2 + \x*\x*\x/6+\x*\x*\x*\x/24+\x*\x*\x*\x*\x/120+\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x/720}); % plot 6th order approx for e^x
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    Note that pulling in <m>x=1</m> in the above polynomial gives us <m>\frac{1957}{720}</m> as an approximation to <m>e^1 = e</m>. In fact,
    <m>\frac{1957}{720} \approx 2.7181</m>, which is an approximation for <m>e</m> that is accurate to 3 decimal places!
  </p>

  <p>
    Before we get too excited, we should note that this procedure that we will develop does not work this well for all functions. For instance, the 
    approximations for the functions <m>\frac{1}{1-x}</m> look like <md>1 + x + x^2 + x^3 + x^4 + \cdots</md>. In fact, if we graph these
    successive approximations, we get the following:
  </p>

  <interactive desmos="ilpy56egwt" width="60%" aspect="2:3" />

  <p>
    What we notice about this is that while adding on more terms does improve the approximations, it only does so for <m>x</m> between <m>-1</m> and <m>1</m>.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    In order to answer questions about how to obtain these approximations, when they are valid, and the errors associated with them, we
    need to develop a general theory of sequences and series. 
  </p>

</section>